C = [
("h1", "9. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "Claim: the volume’s reading, that the oracles against the nations differ in their ground, term and ending, and that the open ones are held open for the Kingdom, answers the objections best made against it, and where an objector is right the volume says so. Modern writers who support parts of the argument are named beside those who press against it. A writer is quoted only where the author has read the words in a free text. Where a position is known only because another writer reports it, the volume says so, names the report, and puts no quotation marks around the position."),
("sub", "9.1 Who agrees, and where the volume differs"),
("p", "Two modern writers support parts of the argument. H. G. L. Peels, in an article in //European Journal of Theology// in 2007 on the oracles in Jeremiah, writes that “four times in Jeremiah 46-51 a word of welfare is added to a word of judgment on a foreign nation.” The four are Jeremiah 46:26, 48:47, 49:6 and 49:39, and they are the clauses on which the open class of this volume rests. Peels also writes that the oracles “unanimously put the acts of God at the centre.” The volume agrees, and adds a point he does not take up, which is the question of what became of the clauses. His article is about the function of the oracles and not their fulfilment."),
("p", "W. A. Criswell, in a sermon of 1975 on Isaiah 19:18–25, reads the passage as “the golden prophecy of the golden millennial tomorrow” and speaks of “the people of the Nile.” He holds Egypt’s blessing to the day of the kingdom and names it, in his words, “a blessing in the midst of the earth.” The volume agrees with him that Isaiah 19:23–25 waits for the Kingdom and that Egypt and Assyria are kept as Egypt and Assyria. It differs in method. His is a sermon, and this volume asks of every oracle what the text itself reports about its term, so the open class is drawn from the clauses and the silence of the later books, not from the verses of one chapter."),
("sub", "9.2 Objection: the oracles were all fulfilled in history"),
("p", "This is the strongest opposing position, and for Tyre it is argued with care. Paul Ferguson, in //Bible and Spade// in 2006, answers critics of Ezekiel 26 and holds that the prophecy was fulfilled as written. He says that “the passage does not state that Nebuchadnezzar would capture the island city and get its wealth,” and that the total destruction of the city was to be accomplished “gradually by one nation after another.” On his reading Ezekiel 26:1–14 and 29:17–20 agree: “there is no contradiction.” Hank Hanegraaff, in //Christian Research Journal// in 2013, holds that the prophecy was fulfilled in stages, with the greatest stroke by Alexander, and writes of Nebuchadnezzar’s siege that “his efforts, however, went largely unrewarded.” The wider claim behind both is that the oracles against the nations are closed."),
("p", "Reply. For Tyre the volume sits near this reading and says so in chapter 6. The grammar of Ezekiel 26 does change from “many nations” to a single “he” to “they,” and the prophet’s own later word in Ezekiel 29:17–20 reports that the first siege had not paid. Where the volume parts from the objection is the move from Tyre to all the nations. A reading that closes every oracle by history must say what happened to the clauses that no later passage reports: the captivity of Moab and of Elam brought again “in the latter days” (Jeremiah 48:47; 49:39), the sons of Ammon returned (Jeremiah 49:6), Egypt “my people” (Isaiah 19:25). Ferguson and Hanegraaff do not argue these, because their subject is Tyre. The reading that a prophecy closes when its first stroke falls is right for Nineveh, whose sentence carried no clause of return, and right for the sentences on Damascus in their terms. It is not right for an oracle that states its own further word."),
("sub", "9.3 Objection: the prophecies failed"),
("p", "A third voice treats the oracle on Tyre as a case of failure. The volume knows this position only because Ferguson reports it in the article above. As he reports it, a line of critical scholarship, among whom he names Carroll, Burtt and Carley, holds that Ezekiel predicted the fall of Tyre to Nebuchadnezzar, that the siege did not deliver it, and that the prophet then retracted the claim in Ezekiel 29:17–20. The volume has not read these writers, describes the position through Ferguson’s report, and puts it in no quotation marks. Ferguson’s own summary of the objection is that Ezekiel 29:17–20 is “an apology by the prophet for being wrong.”"),
("p", "Reply. The objection reads Ezekiel 29:17–20 as a confession, and the verses do not say so. They report that Nebuchadrezzar served “a great service against Tyrus,” that “yet had he no wages, nor his army, for Tyrus,” and that Egypt is given to him as the wages for his army (Ezekiel 29:18, 19). The verse reports what the siege did and did not bring, and what the LORD gives in its place. Nothing in the text withdraws Ezekiel 26. The volume holds the two chapters side by side and does not harmonize them beyond what the text says: Ezekiel 26 and Isaiah 23:15–18 stand together with no timeline the passages do not give. The charge of failure assumes the prophecy of Ezekiel 26:7–11 was a promise of what one king would take, and the chapter’s grammar says otherwise."),
("sub", "9.4 Objection: the open clauses are the church"),
("p", "The Ligonier Ministries magazine //Tabletalk// printed an unsigned reading of Isaiah 19:18–25 in March 2013, titled “Egypt and Assyria Serve the Lord.” It says that “humanly speaking, it was unthinkable for even one city to serve the LORD because pagan Egypt hated him so,” and that the image of five converted cities shows how miraculous the conversion would be. It reads the altar in the midst of the land as “a spiritual altar, a gospel church, in the midst of the land of Egypt.” The objection is that the blessing of Egypt and Assyria is the conversion of the nations to Christ, and that the open clauses are therefore met in the church."),
("p", "Reply. The volume grants the miracle and the wonder at it. It parts from the reading in three places. First, the passage keeps three names, and a church that is neither Egypt nor Assyria nor Israel cannot be the third beside them. The text says “Israel be the third with Egypt and with Assyria” (Isaiah 19:24), and a blessing on Israel as a people in a land is not met by a church in which that distinction is gone. Second, the passage speaks of a highway between Egypt and Assyria and of “the midst of the land,” the language of place, in a chapter whose every verse of promise opens with “in that day.” Third, the Body of Christ is in no Israelite covenant, and the passage belongs to Israel’s promised future, in which nations are blessed through the line the LORD restores. The church of this age is not the place the prophecy waits for."),
("sub", "9.5 Objection: all of it is still to come"),
("p", "The opposite objection holds that every oracle is future, so that Nineveh, Damascus and Tyre wait for a last-day fulfilment. No writer is named for it; it is stated in the author’s words. Reply. The texts do not support it. Jonah 3:10 reports that the LORD repented of the evil and “he did it not” for the forty days, and Nahum and Zephaniah 2:13–15 give the later sentence in terms that look to the city’s ruin, with no clause of return. Amos 1:3–5 gives Damascus its sentence and Zechariah 9:1 speaks of the word against Damascus, but neither attaches a clause of return to it. An oracle with no restoration clause and a stated ending is not made open by being stretched. The volume holds the closed class closed for the same reason it holds the open class open: it follows what the passage says."),
("sub", "9.6 Objection: the open clauses are only a manner of speaking"),
("p", "Peels’s observation that the oracles put the acts of God at the centre can be turned into an objection: if the oracles proclaim God’s rule, the welfare clauses are part of that proclamation and not forecasts of what happens to Moab or Elam. The objection is not Peels’s. His article does not say it, and he writes of the clauses as words of welfare added to words of judgment. It is the volume’s own statement of an objection a reader may make. Reply. The clauses are stated as acts of the LORD, with a term: “Yet will I bring again the captivity of Moab in the latter days, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 48:47). A sentence of this form can be read as a manner of speaking only if the sentences of judgment beside it are read the same way, and then the whole oracle becomes a rhetoric of God’s rule with no event in it. That is not how Jeremiah 25:12 treats the seventy years or how Ezekiel 29:17–20 treats Tyre. The volume takes the clauses as statements of what the LORD will do."),
("sub", "9.7 Objection: do the oracles say anything to nations today?"),
("p", "A last objection asks whether the oracles bind or promise anything to a nation now. No writer is named for it. Reply. The oracles are addressed to nations that existed when they were spoken, on grounds that those nations had incurred against the LORD and against Israel. The volume draws a limited point and no more: the grounds, violence, betrayal, gloating, pride and idols, are sins the prophets name, and the LORD’s concern with them is not confined to Israel (Amos 9:7). That is a statement about the grounds. The volume does not turn the sentences into predictions about any nation now, and it does not read the open clauses as promises to any nation but those the text names."),
("ctbl", "The objections and the replies in one table.", [
    ("Objection", "Held by", "Reply in one line"),
    ("All the oracles were fulfilled in history", "Ferguson (2006) and Hanegraaff (2013), for Tyre", "Right for Tyre in part and for Nineveh; no later passage reports the clauses on Moab, Ammon, Elam or Egypt"),
    ("The prophecies failed", "Carroll, Burtt and Carley, as Ferguson reports", "Ezekiel 29:17–20 reports what the siege did not bring; it does not withdraw Ezekiel 26"),
    ("The open clauses are the church", "Tabletalk (2013)", "Three names are kept; the language is of place; the Body is in no Israelite covenant"),
    ("All the oracles are still to come", "none named", "Nineveh and Damascus carry no clause of return; a stated ending is not made open"),
    ("The clauses are a manner of speaking", "none named; a reader’s objection drawn from Peels’s point", "The clauses are stated as acts of the LORD with a term"),
    ("The oracles speak to nations today", "none named", "The grounds are named sins; the sentences and clauses are not turned into forecasts for any nation now"),
  ], [0.3, 0.3, 0.4]),
("sub", "9.8 What the volume takes from the replies"),
("bul", [
    "Peels and Criswell support parts of the argument: the welfare clauses in Jeremiah 46–51 are real, and Isaiah 19:23–25 waits for the Kingdom with Egypt and Assyria kept as themselves.",
    "Ferguson and Hanegraaff are right for Tyre in the main and cannot close the clauses for Moab, Ammon, Elam and Egypt, which they do not treat.",
    "The charge of failure is known only through Ferguson’s report; Ezekiel 29:17–20 reports what the siege did not bring and does not withdraw Ezekiel 26.",
    "The church reading of Isaiah 19 loses the three names, the language of place and the line of Israel’s restoration.",
    "The two opposite errors, all closed and all open, are each answered by the oracles’ own terms, and the Body of Christ is read into no oracle.",
  ]),
]
